<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Users + Profiles' cross-section surface (nobodies-collective/Humans#866, G5 lane 2).
    Carved in PR A ahead of the section move; PR B added the three entities and the write
    interface Base still names.

    A Contracts/ *folder* inside Humans.Users was never an option here. UserInfo is the
    canonical "everything-about-a-person" read model and IUserServiceRead is injected by
    HumansControllerBase, so between them they are named by more than twenty section
    projects, by Humans.UI, and — decisively — by Humans.Application and
    Humans.Infrastructure, which the section itself references. A folder would cycle at
    the assembly level in both directions (design §15 step 5b,
    memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md).

    It holds exactly what lives outside the section: the UserInfo read model and the eight
    projections it nests, the cross-section read interface and its result shapes, the
    service interfaces something outside the section injects, and — the three additions
    PR B forced, each because a *Base* consumer names it and Base cannot reference a
    section:

      User / UserEmail / EventParticipation.  Humans.UI names the User entity twice
      (CityPlanningHub's UserManager<User>, _LoginPartial's SignInManager<User>) and 48
      files across Shell, Humans.UI, CityPlanning, Development and twenty test projects
      spell UserManager<User> / SignInManager<User> / AddIdentity<User, …>. ASP.NET
      Identity's generic surface cannot be hidden, so User is public here rather than
      internal in the section, and its two nav collections drag UserEmail and
      EventParticipation with it (CS0053 otherwise). The other six entities — Profile,
      ContactField, ProfileLanguage, VolunteerHistoryEntry, CommunicationPreference,
      AccountMergeRequest — are internal in Humans.Users/Domain/ as the Camps precedent
      wants.

      The four Users/Profiles enums (UserState, ProfileState, ContactFieldType,
      ContactFieldVisibility), which PR A could not take because Humans.Domain's entities
      declared columns of those types. The entities are here now, so the cycle is gone.

      IUserService and its command/result records.  Peter's ruling was "public on
      Humans.Users, not in the leaf", with the eleven write-calling section projects
      taking a ProjectReference on the section. That is unreachable alongside the §I
      ruling that AccountDeletionService, ExternalLoginService and
      UserParticipationBackfillService stay in Humans.Application as orchestrators: those
      three plus HumanLifecycleService and three Humans.Infrastructure jobs inject
      IUserService from *Base*, and Humans.Users references both Base projects. The
      approved surface is unchanged — IUserService is public either way — only the
      assembly moved, which is design §15 step 5b's own rule ("a consumer in Base forces
      Humans.<Section>.Contracts"). It also means no consumer needed a new
      ProjectReference at all.

    Still NOT here, and staying internal to the section: IAccountMergeService,
    IDuplicateAccountService, IUnsubscribeService, IUnsubscribeTokenProvider,
    IUserEmailProviderBackfillService, IEmailProblemsService, IProfilePictureService and
    the six repository interfaces.

    UserInfo.Create stays here: it takes the eight EF entities, six of which are now
    internal to the section, so the section owns an internal factory
    (Humans.Users.Services.UserInfoFactory) and the record keeps a primitive-only
    Create for Base and the test projects.

    References Humans.Interfaces (IApplicationService, IInvalidator, SurfaceBudgetAttribute,
    GrandfatheredAttribute), Humans.Domain (the shared enums the projections name) and
    Humans.Onboarding.Contracts (OnboardingResult, returned by
    IUserService.ApplyProfileOnboardingMutationAsync — leaf-to-leaf, and
    Humans.Onboarding.Contracts has no project references at all, so it cannot cycle).
    Acyclic: Humans.Domain names only Humans.Interfaces.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Interfaces\Humans.Interfaces.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Domain\Humans.Domain.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Humans.Onboarding.Contracts\Humans.Onboarding.Contracts.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- User : IdentityUser<Guid> — the ASP.NET Identity principal type. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.EntityFrameworkCore" />
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
